# tail — real-world examples

## Show the last 10 lines (default)

```sh
tail access.log
```

## Show the last N lines

```sh
tail -n 50 access.log
```

## Show everything from line N onward

```sh
tail -n +100 access.log
```

## Show the last N bytes

```sh
tail -c 512 access.log
```

## Follow a growing log in real time

```sh
tail -f /var/log/messages
```

## Follow, and survive log rotation

```sh
tail -F /srv/plex/logs/Plex\ Media\ Server.log
```

## Follow several files at once

```sh
tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log /var/log/nginx/error.log
```

## Flag reference

| Flag   | What it does                              |
| ------ | ----------------------------------------- |
| -n N   | last N lines                              |
| -n +N  | start at line N                           |
| -c N   | last N bytes                              |
| -f     | follow the file as it grows               |
| -F     | follow by name (handles rotation)         |
